<section class="band--pale">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Four things we watch for</h2>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card"><h3>Social and emotional skills</h3><p>Sharing, waiting, saying what you need, calming down. Cooperative play with a teacher nearby to help it go well.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Physical development</h3><p>Climbing, balancing, pouring, cutting, drawing. Big movements and small ones, every day, on the turf and in the sand.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Language and literacy</h3><p>Stories, songs and a lot of conversation in English. Phonics starts when a child is curious about letters, not before.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Mathematics and reasoning</h3><p>Counting real things, sorting, noticing shapes and patterns. Maths as something you do with your hands first.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
{% set waveColor = "white" %}{% include "partials/wave.njk" %}

<section class="faq">
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <h2>Questions parents ask</h2>
    <details><summary>Does my child need any English to start?</summary><p>No. Most of our youngest children arrive with none. They pick it up from the room.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can we start with a few days a week?</summary><p>Most children start gently. For the first few days a parent stays for outdoor time in the morning, then takes their child home. After that come shorter days, building up to the full day as your child settles. Once settled it is a full-time program, and tuition is the same however many days your child comes. We take children from 20 months.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What about nappies and naps?</summary><p>Nappies are fine. Please send spare nappies and wipes, a change of clothes and a plastic bag. There is a two-hour nap every day, and each child has their own cot. The day ends at 15:15, and after-care until 16:30 is available for an extra fee.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is there a progress report?</summary><p>Yes. Early Years families get a written progress report describing what their child is exploring and what comes next.</p></details>
  </div>
</section>

